Why mojo_net's HTTP server was slower than hyper, and how the gap closed
=======================================================================

Notes for whoever takes on the gap. Measured on 2026-10-27 at commit
28616bf, on an i5-1145G7 laptop (Linux 6.12, Mojo 1.2.0, rustc 1.99.1,
hyper 1.12.0). Everything here can be measured again with the commands in
section 2. Facts are marked as measured; estimates say so.

Update, later on 2026-10-27 (the third http review, HR3): the codecs'
instructions per request fell by 32-42% (bench/http/codec.mojo; the
details are in bench/http/README.md). Most of the "line 0" cost in
section 3 was IoError: passed in registers, it made every typed raise
cost ~85 instructions on success. It is passed in memory now. The figures
below are from before that work.

Update, 2026-10-28 (the server review, SR2; docs/server.md): the profile
taken again, mojo and hyper in one run (out/profile-20261028-020523.json):
mojo 4,227 user-space instructions a request pipelined, hyper 4,565. From
the 9,644 below, HR3's work (IoError in memory, cheaper case-insensitive
compares, raw stores for heads, the Host bitmap) left 6,417. Then SR2,
each step measured:
- field bytes copied as 16/32-byte vectors (Headers._copy): 6,417 -> 5,727;
- the review's new hooks and checks (admit, body_limit, the body budget,
  completed, ...): 5,727 -> 6,071, 60 of it a trap: `var error = Error()`,
  made ahead to hold a caught error, takes a stack trace
  (KGEN_CompilerRT_GetStackTrace) on every request. Handle the error
  inside `except e:` instead;
- then, together: 6,071 -> 4,227. The default Content-Type set without
  checks (Headers._checked had been 612 a request); the Date and Server
  lines encoded once a second (DateCache.lines) and the body's length
  handed to the codec as a number (ServerConnection._respond), so neither
  is pushed as a field, encoded and parsed back; Request.path() as a byte
  scan (StringSpan.find had been 125); Expect read from the codec's
  summary; no empty trailer section built per response; the reason
  swapped into the head, not copied; the stack trace gone.
Then timed under wrk the same day (bench/server/README.md, Results;
out/20261028-105727.json, 2 reps of 60 s): pipelined on one CPU, mojo
takes 0.84 us of CPU a request against hyper's 0.83 (it was 1.31 against
0.85), 1,185k req/s against 1,193k (it was 762k against 1,169k); on four
CPUs pipelined, 2,153k against 2,073k. With keep-alive, the top four are
within a few percent, as most of a request is the kernel's loopback TCP.
The gap this note was written about is closed; what is left below is the
history of how it was found, and the leads not taken yet (section 4).


1. The short version
--------------------

- With keep-alive on one CPU, mojo is 14% behind hyper (156k against
  181k req/s). Pipelined, it is 35% behind (762k against 1,169k req/s).
  Source: bench/server/README.md, 60 s runs.
- The kernel's time per request is the same for both (measured): 3.59
  against 3.57 us with keep-alive, 0.33 and 0.33 us pipelined. Both make
  the same system calls.
- mojo's own code takes about twice hyper's time (measured): user time
  1.63 against 0.97 us with keep-alive, 0.99 against 0.52 us pipelined.
- In instructions (measured, callgrind, pipelined, one CPU), mojo runs
  9,644 user-space instructions a request and hyper 4,565.
- The server loop is not the cause: src/server/server.mojo is 699 of
  mojo's instructions (7%).
- Memory allocation is not the cause: mojo allocates nothing per request.
  hyper spends 469 instructions a request in malloc and free.
- The cause is src/http: 5,385 instructions a request. That is more than
  all of hyper's work, for this one request and response.
  - Each head is walked several times.
  - Header names are compared one byte at a time, folding case.
  - Field bytes are checked one byte at a time.
  - Short pieces are copied through the vectorized memcpy's scalar tail.
  - The standard library's bounds checks run in the hot loops.


2. How to measure (before and after a change)
---------------------------------------------

The primary metric is instructions per request. callgrind counts them,
nearly the same from run to run, so a change of 1% shows. Time-based
benchmarks on this shared laptop vary by several percent.

    pixi run -e bench bench-profile                  # builds, runs mojo and hyper, ~3 min
    pixi run -e bench bench-profile --servers mojo   # mojo only
    pixi run -e bench bench-profile --analyze bench/server/out/callgrind.mojo.<time>.1 --requests <n>

- bench-profile runs bench/server/plaintext_profile.mojo under callgrind:
  - one CPU;
  - 16 connections, 16 pipelined requests per write;
  - 3 s of warm-up, then 10 s counted.
- It prints instructions per request: by object, by source file, and by
  function with each function's costliest lines.
- It writes out/profile-<time>.json, which keeps the requests counted. The
  callgrind file stays in out/.
- callgrind_annotate cannot read Mojo's symbols: everything lands under
  "(below main)". Use profile.py's report, or kcachegrind.
- plaintext_profile.mojo has to keep the same handler as plaintext.mojo.
  It exists because valgrind cannot load the program that Stop
  re-executes.
- Both servers are built for x86-64-v3 for the profile, since valgrind has
  no AVX-512. Line numbers in optimized code are approximate, and line 0
  means no line information.

Then confirm in time:

    pixi run -e bench bench-server build
    pixi run -e bench bench-server run --scenarios 1cpu-pipelined,1cpu-keepalive --servers mojo,hyper --duration 20

For an A/B of two mojo builds, alternate them in one session. Build the
old one to another path, then swap the binary path. The A/B notes in
bench/server/README.md, under "Settings tried", show the method. The
machine is shared, so look at the "others" column, and expect ±3% noise
between runs.

Before committing a change to http or server, run:

    pixi run test http/
    pixi run test server/
    pixi run ci

The differential oracles (tests/http/head_oracle.py, chunked_oracle.py)
and the regression tests (tests/http/test_*_regressions*.mojo,
tests/server/test_server_regressions*.mojo) guard the strictness rules
below.


3. What runs for each request (pipelined, instructions a request)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

These are self costs, from out/profile-20261027-150833.json (342,160
requests). Inlined code is counted in the function it was inlined into.

Request side: the codec takes a request out of its input.

  _Conn.dispatch (server.mojo:916)                                    1,553
      inlined: the codec's poll (server.mojo:928), the HeadParser calls
      (parse.mojo:527, 532), the body decoder's restart
      (connection.mojo:793), swap of the event (swap.mojo, ~130)
  HeadParser._find_end (parse.mojo)                                     397
      finds the blank line, already with SIMD (_first, parse.mojo:310)
  HeadParser._parse_fields (parse.mojo)                                 598
      splits the fields, checks every name byte (tchar) and value byte
  _summarize (connection.mojo:336), called at connection.mojo:768       ~340
      walks the fields again for Host, Connection, Content-Length,
      Transfer-Encoding, Upgrade and Expect; it compares names with
      eq_ignore_case (headers.mojo:105), one byte at a time with
      lower_ascii (headers.mojo:98-99: 201 instructions a request)
  _valid_host (connection.mojo:441), called at connection.mojo:773      593
      a byte loop over "127.0.0.1:<port>" (about 15 bytes, so about 40
      instructions a byte); bounds checks are 150 of the 593
      (check_bounds.mojo:62-65, span.mojo:362)
  two Strings for method and target, built from the head's bytes
      (String.__init__ with two _iadd each; with ResponseHead's reason,
      three Strings a request cost ~290 together: lead H)

Response side: the handler, then the server, then the codec.

  Response.text -> Response.bytes (server.mojo:387, 396)
    Headers.set("Content-Type", ...) (headers.mojo:294)                 269
      _checked (headers.mojo:313): is_token over the name, and
      is_field_byte over the value, a byte at a time                    707
      (headers.mojo:79: 156, 89: 125, 319: 100)
      _find: searches existing fields with eq_ignore_case
      _store (headers.mojo:357): two small unsafe_memcpy                547
    body.extend(body): a copy of the 13-byte body
  _Conn.send (server.mojo:1119)                                         567
    ResponseHead(status, reason): copies the reason into a String, even
      when it is empty (parse.mojo:182)
    _scan (server.mojo:1184): walks the fields for Date, Server and
      framing, with eq_ignore_case again
    Date and Server pushed without checks (_push: good), and
      Content-Length by _push_decimal (server.mojo:1202), unchecked
  ServerConnection.send_response (connection.mojo:883)                  908
    _check_reason; then _summarize(head.headers) again                 ~340
      (connection.mojo:897; that is the other half of _summarize's 684)
    _content_length: parses the Content-Length the server just wrote
    String == "CONNECT", == "HEAD" comparisons (two a request)
    _status_line_bytes (connection.mojo:562)                            328
    Headers.encode (headers.mojo:579)                                   819
      encoded_len walks the fields, then two unsafe_memcpy per field
      (memory.mojo:182-183, vectorize.mojo:127, range.mojo:129: the
      vectorized copy's scalar tail, run for every short piece)
  send_body (connection.mojo:986)                                       236
  end_response (connection.mojo:1006)                                   154
  ByteBuffer.write_bytes (buffer.mojo:352)                              291

The standard library adds, across all of the above:

  memory.mojo (copies)                    647
  check_bounds.mojo (index checks)        470
  string.mojo (String building)           444
  range.mojo (range loops)                383
  span.mojo                               218
  list.mojo                               179
  vectorize.mojo                          169
  swap.mojo                               130

hyper, for comparison:

  httparse (head parsing)                ~270
  HeaderMap (building the response's fields)  ~850
  hyper's h1 role, conn, dispatch and io ~850
  the tokio task                          ~930
  malloc and free                          469

hyper does not parse the Host field. Its standard header names
(CONTENT_TYPE) are constants, never checked at run time, and its Date
field comes from a cache (as mojo's DateCache does).


4. Leads, roughly in order of instructions saved per unit of risk
-----------------------------------------------------------------

The savings are estimates from the profile. None of these has been tried.

A. Do not check, search or re-summarize what is valid by construction.
   - Response.bytes calls headers.set("Content-Type", content_type) for
     every response, so the default content type is checked and searched
     each time.
     - Fix: push a known-valid default without checks.
     - Fix: check a caller's content type, but only once (or only when it
       differs from the last one).
     - Saves up to ~1,000 (set's search and _checked); _store's copy
       stays.
   - _Conn.send's _scan and send_response's _summarize walk the same
     response fields, one after the other. send_response also parses the
     Content-Length the server just wrote.
     - Fix: let the server hand the codec what it knows. One option is an
       internal send_response variant that takes the framing (length or
       chunked) and the fields' summary.
     - The public send_response must keep checking a caller's head.
     - Saves ~400-500.
   - ResponseHead(status, reason) copies an empty reason into a String.
     - Fix: skip the copy when it is empty, or keep a StringSlice to a
       static phrase.
     - Saves ~100.

B. One pass over the request head.
   - _summarize is a second walk, done after _parse_fields. The parser
     already looks at every name; it could note the framing fields as it
     stores them.
   - Fix: compare names by length first, as _summarize does. Fill a
     _Summary in _parse_fields, or keep a small "known field" id per
     field.
   - _summarize, _scan and Headers._find then become integer compares
     wherever the id is known.
   - Saves ~300-600 on the request; the same ids also help A.
   - Keep: the summary's rules (duplicate Host, duplicate Content-Length,
     Transfer-Encoding with Content-Length, and so on) are the request
     smuggling defence. Same decisions, fewer walks; the oracles must
     agree.

C. Faster case-insensitive name comparison (eq_ignore_case,
   headers.mojo:105).
   - Each name byte costs several instructions: lower_ascii on both
     sides, a bounds check on each index, and the loop. _summarize is 684
     a request for about 20 compared name bytes, over its two calls.
   - Fix: compare bytes exactly first (most names arrive in canonical
     case, the literals do not). Or compare 8 bytes at a time with
     (x ^ y) & 0xDF... and confirm the differing bytes are letters.
   - Or: lowercase the literal at compile time and fold only the input.
   - This helps _summarize, _scan and _find everywhere.

D. _valid_host (connection.mojo:441).
   - 593 instructions for 15 bytes.
   - Fix: index through the pointer, as parse.mojo does; the loop bound
     already makes it safe. Test reg-name bytes with a 256-bit mask, as
     is_tchar does (_TCHAR_LO/_TCHAR_HI).
   - Probably ~100 after that.
   - Also: on a keep-alive connection, Host rarely changes. Remembering
     the last valid Host bytes per connection and comparing them (a
     memcmp) would skip the parse. The check must still run whenever the
     bytes differ.

E. Small copies.
   - unsafe_memcpy with a run-time count goes through vectorize. For the
     4 to 30-byte pieces of a head, that is its scalar tail, one byte at a
     time: memory.mojo (647 a request) and vectorize.mojo (169), with part
     of range.mojo's 383, spread over encode, _store and write_bytes.
   - Fix: a copy helper for short lengths, with fixed 8/16/32-byte
     overlapping loads and stores (the destination has room:
     ensure_writable came first).
   - Or: avoid the copies. Pre-encode the server's constant field lines
     ("Server: mojo-net\r\n", and Content-Type when it is the default)
     once, and write the line whole.
   - The Date line can come from DateCache already encoded, as a whole
     line.

F. SIMD field checks.
   - _checked and _parse_fields test is_field_byte and is_tchar one byte
     at a time.
   - is_field_byte is two vector compares:
     b >= 0x20 and b != 0x7F, or b == 0x09.
   - is_tchar needs a table lookup (the nibble-shuffle method) or a range
     test.
   - parse.mojo's _first[stop_v, stop_b] (parse.mojo:310) already pairs a
     vector predicate with a scalar one: reuse it.
   - Most fields are short (under 32 bytes), so measure: the win is in
     longer values (cookies, user agents), less in this benchmark.

G. Bounds checks in hot loops.
   - check_bounds.mojo: 470 a request (4.9%).
   - Measured: -D ASSERT=none for the whole program is 5% faster
     pipelined. It was left on: Rust keeps its checks, and this repo
     prefers safety by default.
   - Fix: in the loops above, where the loop bound proves the index safe,
     use p[unsafe_offset=i] on unsafe_ptr(), as parse.mojo does.
   - Keep the checks wherever an index comes from input.

H. Strings per request.
   - Three are built per request: method and target in RequestHead, and
     ResponseHead.reason. Each goes through String(...) with two appends.
   - Fix: the method could be an enum, or a small inline value.
   - Fix: the target could be a span into the connection's input
     (careful: the input buffer is compacted and reused; see CLAUDE.md on
     lifetimes).
   - Changing RequestHead is an http API change: docs/http.md, and every
     user.

A rough ceiling, an estimate only: A to E together might remove about
3,000 of the 9,644 instructions, about 30%. That would put mojo's user
time near 0.65-0.7 us pipelined and the total near 1.0 us. That is about
1M req/s on one CPU, still behind hyper's 0.85 us. With keep-alive the
same savings apply per request, but the kernel's ~3.6 us dominates there,
so the gain would be ~10%.


5. What must not change
-----------------------

- docs/http.md's strictness rules:
  - Host checked;
  - one Content-Length, and no Transfer-Encoding beside it;
  - no obs-fold;
  - control bytes refused;
  - methods and names must be tokens;
  - and the rest listed there.
  These are what keep request smuggling and response splitting out. A
  faster path must reach the same decisions; the differential oracles in
  tests/http compare them.
- Values that come from a handler (Headers.set/add from application code)
  must stay checked. Only the server's own fields, and values the server
  checked once before, can skip the checks.
- The public APIs of http (Headers, ServerConnection, RequestHead,
  ResponseHead) are used by server, llm and the examples. An internal fast
  path is cheaper than an API change.
- Do not turn off bounds checks globally (-D ASSERT=none) in the
  benchmark or the package: it was measured (5%) and declined.


6. Mojo 1.2 traps that matter here (more in CLAUDE.md)
------------------------------------------------------

- An Optional of a large struct is slow: its destructor rebuilds the
  value byte by byte, about 100-260 ns per Optional destroyed. The codec
  already fills a reused event (poll(mut ev)); keep it that way.
- Field reassignment in a loop corrupts memory: a local struct with heap
  fields, whose fields are reassigned in a loop after another field's
  last use. Keep the struct alive past the loop.
- Values are destroyed after their last use. A raw pointer into a buffer
  does not keep the buffer alive.
- comptime for unrolls, and runtime indexes into a wide SIMD are slow.
  Keep vectors in registers and index them with constants.
